=======================================================================
  READ THIS FIRST - BETA SOFTWARE
=======================================================================

  * This is a BETA build for developers and test boards. It replaces
    the Btrieve database engine underneath The Major BBS and every
    module you run, so a problem here can affect all of your data.

  * BACK UP YOUR WHOLE BBS DIRECTORY BEFORE INSTALLING (for example,
    copy C:\BBSV10 to C:\BBSV10-BACKUP with the BBS shut down). Check
    that the copy opens and is complete before you go on.

  * Do NOT install this on a production (public) board unless you are
    certain you have a working, recent backup and you are prepared to
    restore it. Try it on a copy of your board first.

  * While this driver is installed, everything your board writes goes
    into new .db files. Your original .DAT files are left exactly as
    they were and are NOT updated. There is no tool yet to copy the
    .db data back into .DAT files: going back to stock Btrieve means
    restoring your backup and losing what changed since you installed.

  * This build (5B) has passed its automated tests but has NOT yet been
    run on a live board. If you want the most-tested build, use
    v26.10.15.4B, which has.

=======================================================================
  WHAT IT IS
=======================================================================

WBTRV32.DLL is a drop-in replacement for the Btrieve 6 WBTRV32.DLL that
The Major BBS v10 ships with. It answers every Btrieve call the same way
stock Btrieve does, but keeps the data in SQLite databases instead of
Btrieve .DAT files. Your data can then be read live with any SQLite
tool while the BBS runs.

The first time each file is opened, its FOO.DAT is converted to a new
FOO.db beside it. The .DAT is never changed. From then on the .db is
the live copy of that file.

It is derived from the wbtrv32 driver of The Major BBS Emulation
Project (MBBSEmu), with compatibility work for The Major BBS v10 and
its add-on modules.

=======================================================================
  WHAT'S NEW IN 5B
=======================================================================

  * Configuration utilities can now work on files while the BBS is
    running. Before, if a utility (or any second program) changed a
    file the BBS had open, the BBS kept seeing the old data, and its
    next write to that file failed with "BTRIEVE ... ERROR 2", which
    shuts the BBS down. The BBS now always sees the latest data.
  * When two programs write the same file at the same moment, the
    second now waits for the first (up to 2 seconds) instead of
    failing.
  * Get Next / Get Previous after deleting the current record continue
    with the next record, as Btrieve does.
  * The log (wbtrv32.log in the BBS directory) is cleaned up:
      - each start writes a header naming the driver version and the
        program that loaded it
      - every line has a date and time
      - normal answers such as "record not found" and "end of file"
        are no longer logged, so anything in the log is worth reading
      - past 1 MB it starts a new log and keeps the old one as
        wbtrv32.old
  * The DLL's file description is now "SQLite driver for The Major BBS
    v10".

=======================================================================
  REQUIREMENTS
=======================================================================

  * The Major BBS v10 (32-bit). This DLL is 32-bit only.
  * Windows with the Microsoft Visual C++ 2015-2022 Redistributable
    (x86). The Major BBS v10 installer already installs it; if Windows
    reports VCRUNTIME140.dll or MSVCP140.dll missing, install
    vc_redist.x86.exe from Microsoft.
  * Free disk space of at least the total size of your .DAT files, for
    the .db copies.

=======================================================================
  INSTALLING
=======================================================================

  1. Shut the BBS down completely, along with any utilities you have
     open (configuration, menu tree, reports).
  2. BACK UP your whole BBS directory, as described above.
  3. In the BBS directory, rename the existing WBTRV32.DLL to
     WBTRV32.DLL.STOCK. Keep it - it is how you go back.
     (The stock Btrieve 6 DLL is 64,000 bytes, dated 9/19/1996.)
  4. Copy WBTRV32.DLL from this package into the BBS directory.
  5. Check it: right-click WBTRV32.DLL > Properties > Details should
     show "SQLite driver for The Major BBS v10" and version
     26.10.15.5B. You can compare SHA256SUMS.TXT with:
       certutil -hashfile WBTRV32.DLL SHA256
  6. Start the BBS. The first start converts every .DAT the BBS opens,
     which takes longer than usual on large files. Each data directory
     gets a "CONVERTED DATABASES" folder with CONVERSIONS.LOG, listing
     every file converted and how many records it holds.
  7. Use the board, and keep an eye on the screen and on wbtrv32.log.

=======================================================================
  UNINSTALLING
=======================================================================

The safe way back (recommended):

  1. Shut the BBS down completely.
  2. Restore the backup you made before installing.
     Anything that changed while this driver was installed is lost.

Going back to stock without a restore (only if you accept losing every
change made while this driver was installed):

  1. Shut the BBS down completely.
  2. Delete WBTRV32.DLL and rename WBTRV32.DLL.STOCK back to
     WBTRV32.DLL.
  3. Delete the placeholder .DAT files this driver created for files
     made while it was installed. They are tiny text files beginning
     "This is not a Btrieve file", and stock Btrieve cannot open them.
     To list them, from PowerShell in the BBS directory:
       Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter *.dat | Where-Object {
         (Get-Content $_.FullName -TotalCount 1) -like
         'This is not a Btrieve file*' }
  4. You may then delete the .db, .db-wal and .db-shm files and the
     "CONVERTED DATABASES" folders.

=======================================================================
  FILES THE DRIVER CREATES
=======================================================================

  FOO.db (and while running FOO.db-wal, FOO.db-shm)
      The live data for FOO.DAT. Never delete these while the BBS runs.
  Placeholder FOO.DAT
      When a module creates a new Btrieve file, the driver also writes a
      small text FOO.DAT, because modules check for the .DAT to decide
      whether the file exists. Leave it in place.
  CONVERTED DATABASES\CONVERSIONS.LOG
      One per data directory: every conversion, with record counts.
  CONVERTED DATABASES\<date-time>\FOO.db
      An earlier FOO.db, kept when a different FOO.DAT was put in its
      place (a module upgrade or a restore) and converted afresh, or
      when a module re-created a file whose .DAT had been deleted.
      Nothing is ever thrown away.
  wbtrv32.log, wbtrv32.old
      Problems only, in the BBS directory.

=======================================================================
  HOW TO TELL WHICH DRIVER IS INSTALLED
=======================================================================

  * Right-click WBTRV32.DLL > Properties > Details. This driver shows
    "SQLite driver for The Major BBS v10" (v26.10.15.4B showed
    "Btrieve-compatible SQLite database driver") and its version.
    Stock Btrieve is 64,000 bytes, dated 9/19/1996.
  * With this driver, .db files appear next to your .DAT files, and
    "CONVERTED DATABASES" folders appear in your data directories.
  * The first line written to wbtrv32.log at each start names the
    driver version and the program that loaded it.
  * A .db file is a SQLite database: its first bytes read
    "SQLite format 3". A stock .DAT is binary Btrieve data.

=======================================================================
  BROWSING THE DATA
=======================================================================

Any SQLite tool can open the .db files, for example:

  * DB Browser for SQLite (free): https://sqlitebrowser.org
  * SQLiteStudio (free): https://sqlitestudio.pl
  * The sqlite3 command-line program: https://sqlite.org

Each .db holds:

  data_t      one row per Btrieve record: id is the record's position,
              data is the raw record (the same bytes, in the same
              layout, as the module's own record structure), and
              key_0, key_1, ... are the record's key values
  keys_t      the key definitions
  metadata_t  record length, page size, fixed or variable records
  source_t    which .DAT the file was converted from

To be safe, open files READ-ONLY (in DB Browser: File > Open Database
Read Only). Never edit a .db while the BBS is running; an edit that
breaks a module's assumptions can take the board down. To look without
any risk, copy the .db (with its .db-wal, if there is one) somewhere
else and open the copy.

=======================================================================
  KNOWN LIMITATIONS
=======================================================================

  * Records with exactly the same key value can be listed in a
    different order than stock Btrieve lists them, for files converted
    from .DAT. Lookups are not affected.
  * Btrieve transactions are supported, but each file is committed
    separately, so a crash in the middle of ending a transaction can
    keep some files' changes and not others.
  * Record locks are accepted and ignored. The Major BBS runs every
    user on one thread, so it does not depend on them.
  * Btrieve files that use data compression cannot be converted.
  * If you do use a configuration utility while the BBS is running,
    try it on a test board first; this is new in 5B.

=======================================================================
  REPORTING PROBLEMS
=======================================================================

If the BBS stops with "BTRIEVE <operation> ERROR <number> ON FILE
<name>", please send:

  * the exact message,
  * wbtrv32.log (and wbtrv32.old if there is one) from the BBS
    directory,
  * the CONVERSIONS.LOG from the data directory of that file,
  * what you were doing, and which module.

=======================================================================
  VERSION HISTORY
=======================================================================

  26.10.15.5B  Other programs can use files while the BBS runs; busy
               wait; Get Next after Delete; log cleanup (above).
  26.10.15.4B  Create writes a placeholder .DAT, fixing "BTRIEVE
               CREATE ERROR 59" from modules that check for the .DAT
               before creating a file (seen with SysOpMUD after a
               cleanup).
  26.10.15.3B  Btrieve transactions (Begin/End/Abort) supported.
  26.10.15.2B  A different .DAT put in place of a converted one is
               converted afresh, keeping the old .db in "CONVERTED
               DATABASES"; conversion log.
  26.10.15.1B  Large Btrieve 6 files whose page tables are moved by
               stray pages convert completely.
  26.10.13.xB  Matching stock Btrieve on record positions, lock biases,
               key ordering and data lengths, found by running boards
               against stock Btrieve call for call.

